  - Concluded the discussion and created a proposal on how to move forward. See comment in issue, and a copy of it below.
    Today at the regular meeting we agreed to move forward with the proposal described by a previous comment by Scott.
    "Use the Reference Modulation Index from the ETSI table instead of Spectral Efficiency Class or the Reference Modulation Scheme.
    Model Reference Modulation Index as an Integer with range of 1-16.
    So move the reference-modulation-index above the choice of coding-modulation-mode making reference-modulation-index a integer rw leaf with range 1-16 (not mandatory)."
    In addition to that proposal we also need a clear description:
    "The reference-modulation-index is the minimum spectrum efficiency class to be fulfilled by the radio link configuration, as defined in ETSI EN 302 217-2.
     The reference modulation index identifies the minimum spectrum efficiency class and the associated characteristics (i.e. system capacity, spectral efficiency class,
     spectrum mask over a given channel separation) to be used (i.e. declared in the licensing process) in the link per link coordination analysis.
     In cases where there are two subclasses A and B, the assumption is that the radio link configuration should fulfill both classes."

